--TEST--
`Core\Validate`'s four structural predicates agree, over a sweep of degenerate subjects, that none of them is anything — while `isAscii` and `isPrintable` are a different question and split the same sweep — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12's first table is six predicates that never throw: a malformed subject
// is answered `false`, not raised. `validate-members.nvst` walks each
// member's own line. This asks one question of all six at once and
// **counts** the answers, so a member that grew its own idea of "empty" —
// PHP's `filter_var` returning the subject rather than a bool, a `ctype_*`
// answering `false` for the empty string — fails here while still looking
// right on its own row.
final class Ask {
    // How many of the four *structural* predicates refuse `$s`. Four is
    // unanimous refusal; anything less names a shape one of them accepts.
    public static function refusals(string $s): int {
        int $no = 0;
        if (!Core\Validate::isEmail($s)) {
            $no = $no + 1;
        }
        if (!Core\Validate::isDomain($s)) {
            $no = $no + 1;
        }
        if (!Core\Validate::isIp($s)) {
            $no = $no + 1;
        }
        if (!Core\Validate::isMac($s)) {
            $no = $no + 1;
        }
        return $no;
    }
}

// Nothing here is an address, a name, an IP or a MAC under any reading: the
// empty string, three subjects that are only whitespace, one holding a NUL,
// and a run of spaces. There is no string literal for a NUL byte other than
// the code-point escape, and `Core\Str::length` reads 3 for that subject, so
// it is genuinely a character in the string rather than a terminator.
array<string> $subjects = ["", " ", "\t", "\n", "a\u{0000}b", "   "];

int $refused = 0;
int $ascii = 0;
int $printable = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $s) {
    $refused = $refused + Ask::refusals($s);
    if (Core\Validate::isAscii($s)) {
        $ascii = $ascii + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Validate::isPrintable($s)) {
        $printable = $printable + 1;
    }
}

// Unanimous: 6 subjects x 4 predicates.
echo "refused ", $refused, " of 24\n";

// The other two are the *encoding* and the *rendering* question, and the
// module's own docs own why the empty string answers `true` to both where
// every `ctype_*` answers `false`. Every subject above is ASCII — a NUL and
// a tab are ASCII bytes — so `isAscii` cannot be the same question as either
// the four above or as `isPrintable`.
echo "ascii ", $ascii, " of 6\n";

// And `isPrintable` splits the same sweep down the middle: the empty string,
// one space and three spaces have no character in general category `Cc`,
// while a tab, a LF and a NUL each do. Three is the count that says the two
// character-class predicates are not one predicate asked twice.
echo "printable ", $printable, " of 6\n";

// The counter can say less than four, so the unanimity above is an answer
// rather than a loop that never looked. Each of these is accepted by the one
// predicate it is written for — except an all-numeric name, which `isDomain`
// accepts as a hostname where PHP's email filter refuses it (this module's
// own docs own that divergence), so a dotted-quad is the one subject two
// structural predicates agree *to* accept.
echo "email ", Ask::refusals("user@example.com"), " ip ", Ask::refusals("192.0.2.1"),
     " mac ", Ask::refusals("00:11:22:33:44:55"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
refused 24 of 24
ascii 6 of 6
printable 3 of 6
email 3 ip 2 mac 3
